00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:00.880
All right, let me start with a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:23.440
entitled, ‘Are vegans at risk for iodine&nbsp;
deficiency?’ Okay, let’s find my video. Okay.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.880
Adequate dietary iodine is required
for normal thyroid function.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.160
In fact, the two thyroid hormones
are named after how many

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.280
iodine atoms they
contain: T3 and T4.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.920
Now, given that iodine is extensively
stored in the thyroid gland itself,

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:43.840
it's not something you
have to get every day,

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.160
but your overall diet needs
to have some good source.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.680
Unfortunately, the common sources
aren't particularly health-promoting:

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:53.840
iodized salt, [and] dairy foods

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:58.640
because iodine-based cleansers
like betadine are used to sanitize

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.880
the udders, which results in some
iodine leaching into the milk.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:05.000
They also add iodine to cattle feed,

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:10.160
and some commercial breads have
iodine-containing food additives.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:14.320
So if you put people on a paleo-type
diet and cut out dairy and table salt,

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.680
they can develop an iodine deficiency,
even though they double

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:20.720
their seafood intake, which
can also be a source.

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:24.160
What about those switching to diets
centered around whole plant foods?

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:26.960
They're also cutting down
on ice cream and Wonder Bread,

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:31.040
and if they're not eating anything from
the sea—seaweed, sea vegetables—

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.680
they can run into the same problem.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.880
Her parents reported striving to
feed her only the healthiest foods.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.080
The 3-year-old only got plant-based,
unsalted, unprocessed foods

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.160
with no vitamin supplementation.
Now that could have been deadly.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.520
With no vitamin B12, those on strictly
plant-based diets can develop

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.160
irreversible nerve damage, but
in this case, a goiter arose first

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.440
due to inadequate iodine intake.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:01.600
Here's another case of veganism
as a cause of iodine deficient

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:05.840
hypothyroidism in a toddler after
weaning. Now before weaning,

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.960
he was fine because his mother
kept taking her prenatal vitamins,

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:11.680
which luckily contained iodine.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.200
Most vegetarians and vegans are
apparently unaware of the importance

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:20.480
of iodine in pregnancy, just as clueless
as their omnivorous counterparts.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.600
The American Thyroid Association and
the American Academy of Pediatrics

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.360
have recommended that women even
just planning on getting pregnant

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:32.240
should ingest a daily supplement that
contains 150 micrograms of iodine, yet

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:38.800
only 60% of prenatal vitamins marketed
in the US contain this essential mineral.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.960
So, in spite of the recommendations,
about 40% lack it. Therefore, it's

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.800
extremely important that pregnant and
breastfeeding women read the labels

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.960
to ensure they're receiving
an adequate amount.

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:54.560
Women of reproductive age have
an average iodine level of 110,

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:58.160
which is fine for nonpregnant
individuals, but we'd really

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.200
like to see at least
150 in pregnancy.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:05.280
It's basically a 24-hour urine test,
in which iodine sufficiency is defined

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:11.120
as 100 mcg/liter of pee in nonpregnant
adults, which your average vegan

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:15.600
fails to reach in the largest study
done to date, out of Boston.

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:19.840
The recommended average daily
intake is 150 mcg/day for most people,

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:23.120
which you can get in like a
cup and a half of cow's milk.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:27.440
Sadly, plant-based milks are
typically not fortified with iodine,

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:33.120
averaging only about 3 mcg/cup.
In the largest systematic study to date,

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.800
although many plant-based milks are
fortified with calcium, they only found

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:41.120
just 3 of 47 fortified with iodine.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:46.800
Those that were had as much as cow's
milk, but those that weren't fell short.

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:50.320
Plant-based milk companies brag about
enriching their milks with calcium,

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:55.360
and often vitamin B12, D, and vitamin A,
but only rarely are attempts made

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.520
to match the iodine content.

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:01.200
The only reason cow's milk has as
much as it does is that they enrich

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.160
the feed, or it comes
dripping off their udders.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.560
So why don't plant-milk
companies add iodine, too?

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:12.240
I was told by a food scientist at
Silk that my carrageenan video

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.560
played a role in them
switching to another thickener.

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:18.880
Hopefully, they'll see this video
and consider adding iodine, too,

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:23.600
or some company will snatch at
the market advantage opportunity.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.680
The researchers conclude that individuals
who consume plant-based milks

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:31.920
not fortified with iodine may be
at risk for iodine deficiency,

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:36.160
unless they consume alternative
dietary iodine sources,

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:48.640
the healthiest of which is sea
vegetables, which we'll cover next.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:54.960
Okay. So to reiterate, one cause&nbsp;
of hypothyroidism—an underactive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.960 --> 00:13:03.760
thyroid gland—is inadequate iodine intake.&nbsp;
An underactive thyroid gland is especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:10.960
important for pregnant women and women even&nbsp;
considering pregnancy. That largest study of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:19.120
vegan iodine status in Boston found most vegans&nbsp;
have insufficient intake. So what’s the best way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:33.520
to get it? What’s the healthiest natural source&nbsp;
of iodine? That’s the name of the next video.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:38.560
Dairy milk supplies between a quarter and
a half of the daily iodine requirement

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:43.040
in the United States, though milk
itself has little native iodine.

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:46.320
The milk iodine content is mainly
determined by factors like

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.080
the application of iodine-
containing teat disinfectants.

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:52.640
The iodine residues in milk
appear to originate

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:55.920
mainly from the contamination
of the teat surface.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:00.480
The teats are sprayed or dipped with
like betadine-type disinfectants,

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:03.360
and the iodine just kind
of leaches into the milk.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:06.800
Too bad most of the plant-
based milks on the market

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:09.040
aren't enriched
with iodine, too.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.640
Fortified soy milk is probably
the healthiest of the plant milks,

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:17.280
but even if it was enriched with iodine,
what about the effects of soy

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:19.040
on thyroid function?

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:23.120
It's funny, when I searched the
medical literature on soy and thyroid,

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:24.720
this study popped up:

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:29.440
a cost-effective way to train
residents to do thyroid biopsies.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:33.840
Just stick the ultrasound probe
right on top and go to town.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:40.240
It turns out on ultrasound your
thyroid gland looks a lot like tofu.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:43.760
Anyway, the idea that soy may
influence thyroid function

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:48.000
originated over eight decades ago when
marked thyroid enlargement was seen

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:53.920
in rats fed raw soybeans, though the
observation that people living in Asian

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:56.160
countries have consumed
soy foods for centuries

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:00.120
with no perceptible thyrotoxic effects
certainly suggests their safety.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.800
The bottom line is there does
not seem to be a problem

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.440
with people who have
normal thyroid function.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.880
However, soy foods may inhibit
the oral absorption of Synthroid,

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:13.600
thyroid hormone replacement
drugs, but so do all foods.

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:17.680
That's why we tell patients to take it
on an empty stomach. But you also

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:21.520
have to be getting enough iodine,
so it may be particularly important

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:26.240
for soy food consumers to make sure
their intake of iodine is adequate.

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.920
What's the best way to get iodine?
For those who use table salt,

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:31.280
make sure it's iodized.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:34.400
Currently, only half of table
salt sold contains iodine,

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:38.160
and the salt used in processed
foods is typically not iodized.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:41.520
Of course, ideally, we
shouldn't add salt at all.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:48.160
Dietary salt is a public health hazard.
Think this title is a little over the top?

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:53.760
Dietary salt is the #1 dietary risk
factor for death on the planet Earth,

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:57.280
wiping out more than
three million people a year

00:15:57.280 --> 00:16:01.360
twice as bad as not
eating your vegetables.

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:04.720
What's the best source of iodine, then?

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:07.280
Sea vegetables! You can get
a little iodine here and there

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:08.640
from a whole variety of foods,

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:12.720
but the most concentrated source
by far, with up to nearly 2,000%

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:14.960
of your daily allowance
in just a single gramâ€”

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:18.320
which is like the weight
of a paperclip: seaweed.

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:22.400
Given that iodine is extensively stored
in the thyroid, it can be safely consumed

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:25.280
intermittently, meaning you
don't have to get it every day,

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:28.080
which makes seaweed use
in a range of foods attractive,

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.800
and occasional seaweed intake
enough to ensure iodine sufficiency.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:36.000
However, some seaweed
should be used with caution

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:40.640
due to its overly high iodine
content, like kelp.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:43.200
Too much iodine can
cause hyperthyroidism,

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:46.640
a hyperactive thyroid gland.
A woman presented

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:49.680
with a racing heartbeat, insomnia,
anxiety, and weight loss

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:54.560
thanks to taking just two
tablets a day containing kelp.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:59.040
In my last video, I noted how the
average urinary iodine level of vegans

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:04.480
was less than the ideal levels, but
there was one kelp-eating vegan

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:07.760
with a urinary concentration over 9,000.

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:10.960
Adequate intake is when
you're peeing out 100 to 199.

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:17.520
Excessive iodine intake is when you
break 300; 9,437: way too much.

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:23.120
The recommended average daily intake
is 150 mcg/day for non-pregnant,

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:28.320
non-breastfeeding adults, and we may want
to stay below 600 on a day-to-day basis,

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:31.760
whereas a tablespoon of
kelp may contain 2,000.

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:34.800
I'd stay away from kelp
because it has too much,

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:39.760
and stay away from hijiki because
it contains too much arsenic.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:43.280
Here's how much common seaweed
preparations should give you

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:46.960
an approximate daily allowance:
two nori sheets, you can literally

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:49.600
just nibble on them
as snacks like I do;

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:53.840
one teaspoon of dulse flakes, which
you can just sprinkle on anything;

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:58.320
one teaspoon of dried arame, which
is great for like adding to soups;

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:02.000
or one tablespoon of seaweed salad.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:06.080
If iodine is concentrated in marine
foods, this raises the question

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:10.640
of how early hominins living in
continental areas could have met

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:12.640
their iodine requirements.

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:16.640
Well, here's what bonobos do,
perhaps our closest relatives.

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:24.400
During swamp visits, they
all forage aquatic herbs.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:31.120
You can munch on aquatic plants yourself&nbsp;
by incorporating seaweed into your diet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:37.760
the healthiest natural source of iodine.

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:48.560
Okay, so we have covered iodine. Okay, hold on.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:56.640
Okay, so we covered thyroid issues caused by&nbsp;
either too little or too much iodine intake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:04.160
but what about hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism&nbsp;
caused by autoimmune diseases like Hashimoto’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:09.200
thyroiditis? That’s what we’ll turn&nbsp;
to next in my video, 'The Best Diet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:30.960
for Hypothyroidism and Hyperthyroidism'.&nbsp;
And then I can finish eating my seaweed.

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:34.480
There are several autoimmune diseases
that affect the thyroid gland,

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:39.680
the most common being Graves' disease
and Hashimoto's thyroiditis.

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:44.560
Graves' disease results in hyperthyroidism,
an overactive thyroid gland.

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:48.480
Though slaughterhouses are
supposed to remove thyroid glands,

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:52.720
should some neck meat slip in,
you can suffer a similar syndrome

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:55.760
called hamburger thyrotoxicosis.

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:58.800
But that's not from your body making
too much thyroid hormone;

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:01.600
that's from your body eating
too much thyroid hormone.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:06.320
Graves' disease is much more common,
and meat-free diets may be able to help

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:09.120
with both, as plant-based diets
may be associated

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:11.600
with a low prevalence
of autoimmune disease

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:15.600
in general, as observed, for example,
in rural sub-Saharan Africa.

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:18.400
Maybe it's because plants
are packed with antioxidants,

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:22.080
which are possible protective factors
against autoimmune diseases.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:25.360
Maybe it's because plants are packed
with anti-inflammatory compounds.

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:29.840
After all, a whole food plant-based diet
is basically synonymous with

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:36.480
an anti-inflammatory diet. But you
don't know until you put it to the test.

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:40.880
It turns out the exclusion of all
animal foods was associated with

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:44.880
half the prevalence of hyperthyroidism
compared with omnivorous diets.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.000
Lacto-ovo vegetarian and
fish-only diets were associated

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:52.800
with intermediate protection, but
a 52% lower odds of hyperthyroidism

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:56.640
among those eating strictly
plant-based diets.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:59.840
This apparent protection may be due
to the exclusion of animal foods,

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:02.720
the benefits of plant foods, or both.

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:05.600
Animal foods, like meat, eggs,
and dairy products, may contain

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:08.080
high estrogen concentrations,
for example,

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:11.120
which have been linked to
autoimmunity in preclinical studies.

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:14.800
Or the decrease in animal protein
may downregulate IGF-1,

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:17.120
which is not just a cancer-
promoting growth hormone,

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:20.880
but may play a role in
autoimmune diseases as well.

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:24.320
Or it could be the good stuff in
plants that may protect cells,

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:28.640
like the polyphenol phytochemicals,
such as flavonoids found in plant foods.

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:31.920
Maybe it's the environmental toxins
that build up in the food chain.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:35.440
For example, fish contaminated with
industrial pollutants, like PCBs,

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:40.880
are associated with increased
frequency of thyroid disorders.

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:45.680
Okay, what about the other autoimmune
thyroid disease, Hashimoto's thyroiditis,

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:49.280
which, assuming you're getting
enough iodine, is the primary cause

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:54.880
of hypothyroidism, an underactive
thyroid gland. Graves' disease

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:59.040
wasn't the only autoimmune disorder
that was rare or virtually unknown

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:03.360
among those living in rural sub-Saharan
Africa, eating near-vegan diets.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:08.240
They also appeared to have less
Hashimoto's. There's evidence

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:12.000
that those with Hashimoto's have
compromised antioxidant status,

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:14.560
but we don't know if
it's cause or effect.

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:17.680
But if you look at the dietary factors
associated with blood levels

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:22.480
of autoimmune anti-thyroid antibodies,
animal fats seem to be associated

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:23.520
with higher levels,

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:27.200
whereas vegetables and other plant
foods are associated with lower levels.

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:30.560
So again, anti-inflammatory
diets may be useful.

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:33.840
No surprise, as Hashimoto's
is an inflammatory disease.

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:38.720
That's what thyroiditis means:
inflammation of the thyroid gland.

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:42.560
Another possibility is the reduction
in methionine intake, an amino acid

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:46.560
concentrated in animal protein, thought
to be one reason why the consumption

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:51.040
of whole plant foods is likely to have
a favorable influence on longevity,

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:54.960
through decreasing the risk of cancer,
heart disease, and diabetes.

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:58.640
And methionine restriction improves
thyroid function in mice,

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:02.880
but it has yet to be put to the test
for Hashimoto's in humans.

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:08.080
If you compare the poop of patients
with Hashimoto's to controls,

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:11.280
the condition appears to be
related to a clear reduction

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:16.320
in the concentration of Prevotella
species. Prevotella are good

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:20.080
fiber-eating bugs known to enhance
anti-inflammatory activities.

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:23.040
Decreased Prevotella levels
are also something you see in

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:27.840
other autoimmune conditions, such as
multiple sclerosis and type 1 diabetes.

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:32.320
How do you get more Prevotella?
Eat more plants.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.920
But put a vegetarian on a diet
of meat, eggs, and dairy,

00:23:35.920 --> 00:23:39.920
and within as few as four days
you can drive down levels.

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:44.480
So one would expect those eating plant-
based diets to have less Hashimoto's,

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:48.880
but in a previous video I expressed
concern about insufficient iodine intake,

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:50.960
which could also lead to hypothyroidism.

00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:55.120
So, which is it?
Let's find out.

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:58.720
Vegan diets tended to be
associated with lower, not higher,

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:03.200
risk of hypothyroid disease.
Why the word "tended"?

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:06.880
Because the associated protection against
hypothyroidism incidence and prevalence

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:10.720
studies did not reach
statistical significance.

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:12.800
It wasn't just because they were slimmer.

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:14.960
The lower risk existed even after

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:18.640
controlling for body weight, so
they think maybe it's because

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:21.360
animal products may induce inflammation.

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:25.440
The question I have, though, is: if
someone who already has Hashimoto's,

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:28.000
I mean, what happens
if you change their diet?

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:34.800
That's exactly what I'll explore next.

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:42.160
Okay. So that was about preventing hypothyroidism&nbsp;
or hyperthyroidism, preventing Graves’ disease and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:51.360
Hashimoto’s. But what about a dietary intervention&nbsp;
to treat Hashimoto’s, treat hypothyroidism? That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.360 --> 00:25:06.400
what we’ll close out with before I take questions:&nbsp;
a natural treatment for Hashimoto’s disease.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:11.680
Autoimmune thyroiditis, also known as
Hashimoto's disease, is an organ-specific

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:15.840
autoimmune disorder where your body
attacks your own thyroid gland,

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:20.000
often leading to hypothyroidism
due to destruction and scarring

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:21.680
of the gland itself.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:25.200
We know there's a genetic component,
since identical twins are more likely to

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:30.480
share the disease than fraternal twins.
However, even with identical twins,

00:25:30.480 --> 00:25:35.680
the concordance rate was only about 50%,
meaning even if your identical twin,

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:40.080
with basically the exact same DNA as you,
has the disease, there's only like

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:45.280
a flip of a coin's chance you'll get it,
emphasizing that other important factors

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:49.680
other than your genes play a role
in the development of the disease.

00:25:49.680 --> 00:25:54.720
Genes load the gun, but
environment may pull the trigger.

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:58.160
More than 90 chemicals have been
noted disrupting hormonal balance

00:25:58.160 --> 00:26:01.680
or thyroid function; however, only
a few such pollutants show evidence

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:04.880
that they contribute to
autoimmune thyroid disease.

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:08.400
These include polyaromatic
hydrocarbons, which smokers

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:10.080
get a lot from their cigarettes,

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:14.720
but in nonsmokers, exposure
comes almost entirely from food.

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:18.160
Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons are
primarily formed when muscle meats,

00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:21.040
such as beef and pork, fish,
or chicken, are cooked

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:23.760
by high temperature methods
such as grilling.

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:28.880
PBBs are a type of flame-retardant
chemical no longer manufactured

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:31.840
in the U.S., but still found
in the aquatic food chain.

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:36.240
And PCBs are used in a number
of industrial processes and end up

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:39.760
in peoples' bodies largely through
the consumption of fish as well,

00:26:39.760 --> 00:26:42.240
but also other meat and eggs.

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:45.920
So one might suspect those eating plant-
based diets would have lower rates

00:26:45.920 --> 00:26:49.600
of hypothyroidism, and indeed,
despite their lower iodine intake,

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:52.000
vegan diets tended to be protective.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:55.680
But they've never been put to
the test in an interventional trial.

00:26:55.680 --> 00:27:00.960
A modification of the Paleolithic diet
has been tried in Hashimoto's patients,

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:04.080
but didn't appear to improve
thyroid function.

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:08.080
What did, though, was Nigella Sativa.

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:12.480
That name should sound familiar to anyone
who's read my latest book "How Not to Diet"

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:16.880
or watched my latest live presentation
"Evidence-Based Weight Loss."

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:22.800
Nigella Sativa is the sciency name
for black cumin, which is just

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:27.920
a simple spice, but it's also used
for a variety of medicinal purposes.

00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:32.240
In this study, a randomized, double-blind
placebo-controlled trial of eight weeks

00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:36.960
of a half teaspoon of powdered black
cumin a day in Hashimoto's patients.

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:40.320
Not only was there a significant
reduction in body weight,

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:44.960
which is why I profiled it in the book,
it also significantly reduced thyroid

00:27:44.960 --> 00:27:48.960
stimulating hormone, a sign that
thyroid function was improving.

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:53.280
It even lowered the level of autoimmune
anti-thyroid antibodies, as well as

00:27:53.280 --> 00:27:57.520
increasing blood levels of thyroid
hormone T3 in these Hashimoto's patients.

00:27:58.160 --> 00:28:01.120
In addition, there was a significant
drop in Interleukin 23,

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:05.680
a proinflammatory cell signal thought to
help promote the autoimmune inflammation

00:28:05.680 --> 00:28:11.440
of the thyroid, so further confirming
the anti-inflammatory nature of the plant.

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:16.560
And what were the side effects?
Well, a 17% drop in LDL bad cholesterol.

00:28:16.560 --> 00:28:19.680
Given the fact that patients with
Hashimoto's may be at particularly

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:23.120
high risk of developing heart
disease, this is exactly

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:25.360
the kind of side effect we'd want.

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:27.840
Considering these health-promoting
effects of black cumin,

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:29.920
it can be considered as
a therapeutic approach

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:34.560
in the management of Hashimoto's-
related metabolic abnormalities.

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:37.280
A similar trial failed
to find a benefit though.

00:28:37.280 --> 00:28:42.400
Same dose, same time frame, but no
significant changes in thyroid function.

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:46.320
In contrast with the last study, though,
they were not all Hashimoto's patients,

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:51.120
but rather hypothyroid for any reason,
and that may have diluted the results.

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:54.480
And it's possible that telling patients
to take the black cumin doses

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:58.080
with their thyroid hormone replacement
therapy may have interfered

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:01.360
with its absorption, similar
to other foods and drugs,

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:05.360
which is why patients are normally
told to take it on an empty stomach.

00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:08.800
Since there are no downsides—
I mean, it's just a simple spice—

00:29:08.800 --> 00:29:10.640
I figure, why not give it a try?

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:17.120
The worst that can happen
is you'll have tastier food.

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:28.240
So, a half teaspoon of powdered black cumin seeds&nbsp;
once a day on an empty stomach for Hashimoto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:35.600
sufferers. For those of you watching who have this&nbsp;
disease, try it out. Let me know how it works.

00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:41.600
Okay, it is time for me to take questions.&nbsp;
Let me just give NutritionFacts staff a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:48.720
minutes to compile them into a list for me. As&nbsp;
you can imagine, with thousands of attendees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:54.080
there are a gazillion questions, and lots&nbsp;
of them are duplicates. So you can try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:57.200
find out what the most common questions are&nbsp;
and put them all together into one question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:02.960
rather than just asking questions&nbsp;
that fewer people are interested in.

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:08.240
Okay. So if you have not already clicked&nbsp;
